// Type-checking for this package's TESTS.
//
// The build config deliberately narrows `include` to `src/**/*`, so nothing ever
// type-checked `tests/`: a fixture missing a newly-required field, or holding a
// wrongly-typed value, produced NO error from `type-check` or `lint` (the ESLint
// test override runs with `projectService: false`) and Vitest strips types without
// checking them. Such a fixture failed only if a runtime assertion happened to
// notice, which is how several of them had to be hunted down by running the suite
// instead of by the compiler.
//
// A SEPARATE config rather than widening `include` in the build config, because
// that config also drives `tsc` emit: adding `tests/` there would compile test
// files into `dist/`.
//
// Strictness is inherited wholesale from the build config (and through it from
// `tsconfig.base.json`) and must stay that way — a test fixture is exactly where a
// weakened `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` or `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` would hide
// the drift this config exists to catch.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    // `composite` requires declaration emit and every input under `rootDir`;
    // neither holds for a check-only pass that also reads `tests/`.
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    // The repo root, not `.`: `rootDir` is a pure CONSTRAINT under `noEmit`, and
    // `tests/determinism.ts` re-exports the repo-wide determinism harness in
    // `tests/harness/`, which every package's suite must pin identically (three
    // copies of that contract would drift, and a package that quietly stopped
    // pinning `LC_ALL` would start depending on the developer's desktop locale).
    // Same reasoning as `packages/server/tsconfig.test.json`.
    "rootDir": "../..",
    // Incremental build info, and the one place its rationale is written out; the
    // other check-only configs point here.
    //
    // `type-check` runs SEVEN separate tsc invocations, and without build info each
    // one re-reads and re-checks every file from scratch on every commit: measured
    // at 1m 12s for the gate, against a 90-second budget for the whole tier. With
    // build info an unchanged run reuses the previous program and re-reports its
    // stored diagnostics rather than recomputing them, measured at 25s.
    //
    // Two rules, and both are load-bearing:
    //
    //   * ONE FILE PER INVOCATION. Two programs sharing a build-info file overwrite
    //     each other's state on every alternate run, which costs the whole speedup
    //     and reads, from the outside, exactly like the feature working.
    //   * NEVER ON A CONFIG THAT EMITS. `packages/{shared,server,client}/tsconfig.json`
    //     drive `npm run build` as well as `type-check`, and an incremental NON-BUILD
    //     `tsc` does not check whether its outputs still exist (typescript 6.0.3
    //     guards that loop with `if (!isIncremental)`, and only in build mode at
    //     that) — so `dist/` deleted while the build info survived makes the build
    //     exit 0 having emitted NOTHING, and it silences `tsc -b`'s own
    //     output-existence check for the client. Measured, both ways. The three
    //     checking passes therefore take their build info from a
    //     `--tsBuildInfoFile` flag in each package's `type-check` script instead,
    //     which is why only the check-only configs carry these two keys.
    //
    // `.cache/` is gitignored (`.gitignore:27`), the name is separately covered by
    // `*.tsbuildinfo` (`.gitignore:7`), `.dockerignore` excludes both, and
    // `npm run clean` removes it — this file describes one machine's tree state,
    // and a stale one shadowing an image's own build would be worse than useless.
    "incremental": true,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "../../.cache/tsbuildinfo/shared.tests.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*", "tests/**/*"]
}
